From 58822da5db3c7524e7203c7281d5cb8fb8b39408 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 13:28:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/29] Count a bad session END as a fatal boot, and ask pstore
 what the kernel wrote

The field log from the device closes the loop and exposes a hole in it. The
watchdog did its job: last frame at 10:15:28.6, budget blown at 2154 ms, abort at
10:15:31.2 - 300 ms inside the kernel's own 3 s fence timeout - tree swept,
session ended. The phone restarted 48 s later anyway, which settles what the
guard can and cannot do: the bus fault is committed before userspace sees the
stall, so no timing wins that race. What remains is refusing the next attempt.

The next attempt was not refused, and that is the bug. `noteSessionEnded` had
already cleared the open-session mark, so at the next boot `interruptedSession`
found nothing open, the boot reason was unreadable as always, and the guard
logged "no boot reason and no interrupted session" and left the latch alone. A
check that only looks for a session still running at boot cannot see a crash the
guard cleaned up after.

GpuSafetyPrefs now keeps how the last session ended, and a new boot preceded
within ten minutes by an abnormal end - or by a guard abort, which is written at
the moment it is decided, because the teardown is exactly when the kernel may
take the phone - is a fatal boot. A false lock costs one tap on the Safety page;
a false clear costs the device, so a wall clock that moved backwards counts as
recent rather than as long ago.

BootReason gains a third witness: pstore, the console tail the kernel leaves in
RAM for the boot after a panic. It is usually root-only, so nothing here assumes
it is readable; what it guarantees is that the panel tells you which kind of
blind you are, "no pstore buffer present" or "the buffer exists and this app
cannot read it", because only one of those has a fix.

Measured limit and the missed latch are both written up in docs/gpu-safety.md
under honest limits, with the log lines, and in the progress log.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
